{
 "thread": "EG0RMMdBBeU",
 "subject": "modeling spatial structure in HMs",
 "messages_listed": 3,
 "last_activity": "2018-11-16T16:56:43Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "40e8c-hvBAAJ",
   "from": "Kery Marc",
   "author": "00ebeb6e0521112d",
   "date": "2018-11-12T10:29:11Z",
   "subject": "modeling spatial structure in HMs",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<div lang=\"EN-US\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\">\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Dear all,<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Apologies for shameless self-PR here, but I think here’s a paper of some general (methodological) interest because it shows how spatial structure can easily be incorporated in HMs of the kinds that we talk\r\n about on this list. My colleagues had an observational study with a split-plot design: where there are nested levels of observation, with some covariates measured on one level and some on another. Just ignoring this inherent dependency would be bad (e.g.,\r\n “pseudoreplication” and all that). The traditional way in mixed-model and ANOVA land to accommodate such spatial structure is to fit a random block effect to account for increased similarity of plots within the same block as opposed to those lying in different\r\n blocks. This is of course easily achieved in BUGS and here is an example inside of an abundance-based community model (i.e., a DRY model according to Chapter 11 in AHM1).<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">To view the online publication, please click here:<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><a href=\"http://journal.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fevo.2018.00175/full?&amp;utm_source=Email_to_authors_&amp;utm_medium=Email&amp;utm_content=T1_11.5e1_author&amp;utm_campaign=Email_publication&amp;field=&amp;journalName=Frontiers_in_Ecology_and_Evolution&amp;id=349063\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://journal.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fevo.2018.00175/full?%26utm_source%3DEmail_to_authors_%26utm_medium%3DEmail%26utm_content%3DT1_11.5e1_author%26utm_campaign%3DEmail_publication%26field%3D%26journalName%3DFrontiers_in_Ecology_and_Evolution%26id%3D349063&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792494601232000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1SHuEahB-12oXbeGmqiDky\">http://journal.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fevo.2018.00175/full?&amp;utm_source=Email_to_authors_&amp;utm_medium=Email&amp;utm_content=T1_11.5e1_author&amp;utm_campaign=Email_publication&amp;field=&amp;journalName=Frontiers_in_Ecology_and_Evolution&amp;id=349063</a><u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;color:black\">Of course, we’re not the first ones to do this, e.g., David Miller has a paper around 2013 where they accommodate such a blocking structure in a single-species occupancy model (I believe) and no\r\n doubt there must be others. But my point here is just to (1) emphasize that often we have such internal structure that is induced by the design of our study and (2) we can easily account for the resulting spatial structure.<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;color:black\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;color:black\">Bestr regards  --- Marc<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Tahoma&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;;color:black\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Tahoma&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;;color:black\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "DdXjx8nBAwAJ",
   "from": "Kery Marc",
   "author": "00ebeb6e0521112d",
   "date": "2018-11-16T08:38:12Z",
   "subject": "RE: modeling spatial structure in HMs",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<div vlink=\"purple\" link=\"blue\" lang=\"EN-US\">\r\n<div style=\"direction:ltr;font-family:Verdana;color:#000000;font-size:10pt\">\r\nHi all,<br>\r\n<br>\r\nmotivated by an email from Jerry Cole, I would like to add the following:<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<font size=\"2\">Blocking is really only the crudest form of accounting for similarity due to spatial proximity. On the other end of the spectrum are geostatistical models, such as these (in the context of &#39;interesting&#39; HMs):<br>\r\n<a href=\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/51171238_Using_multilevel_spatial_models_to_understand_salamander_site_occupancy_patterns_after_wildfire\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/51171238_Using_multilevel_spatial_models_to_understand_salamander_site_occupancy_patterns_after_wildfire&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792494601234000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0Ss8v1d6kkaQBeDuG_b4y-\">https://www.researchgate.net/publication/51171238_Using_multilevel_spatial_models_to_understand_salamander_site_occupancy_patterns_after_wildfire</a><br>\r\n<br>\r\nCAR models and geostatistical models with negative exponential correlation function can be fit with functions in WinBUGS or OpenBUGS, but not JAGS (you&#39;d have to code them up yourself).<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWith BUGS, splines are much more efficient though, see for instance here in the context of a static occupancy model:\r\n<a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1472-4642.2011.00831.x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1472-4642.2011.00831.x&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792494601234000&amp;usg=AOvVaw06ljiEJ8DO24w0eaDgtzSz\">\r\nhttps://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1472-4642.2011.00831.x</a><br>\r\n<br>\r\nIf you are worried that blocking doesn&#39;t remove all the residual spatial autocorrelation, you could conceivably combine block effects (to eliminate larger-scale heterogeneity) with such more fine-scale modeling of residual correlations in the same model.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nFinally, it&#39;s worth mentioning some R packages that can do (static) occupancy or Nmix models with modeling of residual spatial autocorrelation:<br>\r\n- </font><font size=\"2\"><font size=\"2\">For static occupancy models, there is the stocc R package, which does them with CAR and RSR, see\r\n<a href=\"https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1890/12-0564.1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1890/12-0564.1&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792494601234000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2LYCiupe-JOF2WM1CgP_qK\">\r\nhttps://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1890/12-0564.1</a><br>\r\n</font>- For static occupancy and Nmix models, there is the the hSDM package: <a href=\"https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/hSDM/hSDM.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/hSDM/hSDM.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792494601234000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2Ptu8NEvx7fjO7M-iCFFPg\">https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/hSDM/hSDM.pdf</a><br>\r\n<br>\r\nBest regards  ---- Marc<br>\r\n</font><br>\r\n<br>\r\n<div style=\"font-family:Times New Roman;color:#000000;font-size:16px\">\r\n<hr>\r\n<div style=\"direction:ltr\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Tahoma\" color=\"#000000\"><b>From:</b> Kery Marc<br>\r\n<b>Sent:</b> 12 November 2018 11:27<br>\r\n<b>To:</b> <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">hmec...@googlegroups.com</a><br>\r\n<b>Subject:</b> modeling spatial structure in HMs<br>\r\n</font><br>\r\n</div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div vlink=\"purple\" link=\"blue\" lang=\"EN-US\"><div style=\"direction:ltr;font-family:Verdana;color:#000000;font-size:10pt\"><div style=\"font-family:Times New Roman;color:#000000;font-size:16px\">\r\n<div></div>\r\n<div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Dear all,</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"> </span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">Apologies for shameless self-PR here, but I think here’s a paper of some general (methodological) interest because it shows how spatial structure can easily be incorporated in HMs of the kinds that we talk\r\n about on this list. My colleagues had an observational study with a split-plot design: where there are nested levels of observation, with some covariates measured on one level and some on another. Just ignoring this inherent dependency would be bad (e.g.,\r\n “pseudoreplication” and all that). The traditional way in mixed-model and ANOVA land to accommodate such spatial structure is to fit a random block effect to account for increased similarity of plots within the same block as opposed to those lying in different\r\n blocks. This is of course easily achieved in BUGS and here is an example inside of an abundance-based community model (i.e., a DRY model according to Chapter 11 in AHM1).</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"> </span></p>\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\">To view the online publication, please click here:</span></p>\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"><a href=\"http://journal.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fevo.2018.00175/full?&amp;utm_source=Email_to_authors_&amp;utm_medium=Email&amp;utm_content=T1_11.5e1_author&amp;utm_campaign=Email_publication&amp;field=&amp;journalName=Frontiers_in_Ecology_and_Evolution&amp;id=349063\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://journal.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fevo.2018.00175/full?%26utm_source%3DEmail_to_authors_%26utm_medium%3DEmail%26utm_content%3DT1_11.5e1_author%26utm_campaign%3DEmail_publication%26field%3D%26journalName%3DFrontiers_in_Ecology_and_Evolution%26id%3D349063&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792494601235000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3TYuvt0C4c9lxLQFsCqFsA\">http://journal.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fevo.2018.00175/full?&amp;utm_source=Email_to_authors_&amp;utm_medium=Email&amp;utm_content=T1_11.5e1_author&amp;utm_campaign=Email_publication&amp;field=&amp;journalName=Frontiers_in_Ecology_and_Evolution&amp;id=349063</a></span></p>\r\n<p><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt\"> </span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;color:black\">Of course, we’re not the first ones to do this, e.g., David Miller has a paper around 2013 where they accommodate such a blocking structure in a single-species occupancy model (I believe) and\r\n no doubt there must be others. But my point here is just to (1) emphasize that often we have such internal structure that is induced by the design of our study and (2) we can easily account for the resulting spatial structure.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;color:black\"> </span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;color:black\">Bestr regards  --- Marc</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Tahoma&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;;color:black\"> </span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Tahoma&quot;,&quot;sans-serif&quot;;color:black\"> </span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"> </p>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n</div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ihoa3v3cAwAJ",
   "from": "Daniel Hocking",
   "author": "068cbc8bd5fb0d5d",
   "date": "2018-11-16T16:56:43Z",
   "subject": "Re: modeling spatial structure in HMs",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\">Thanks for the info and links Marc. I’ll just also note a recent paper of ours (shameless plug) where we included spatially-explicit random effects (essentially continuous without blocking) in a hierarchical abundance model: <a href=\"https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/eap.1767\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/eap.1767&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792494601246000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1Z3UVZNcX5KYZwsiH32-v_\">https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/eap.1767</a><div><br></div><div>We did it in a frequentist framework rather than Bayesian for practical purposes using TMB, which employs fast automatic differentiation. Our simulations and analyses are specific to stream networks but the same approach could be employed for 2D space using Gaussian Markov Random Fields with a triangulation mesh and Matern correlation function with distance. This is a similar approach to INLA (which package can still be used to create the mesh). With INLA it’s difficult to get the hierarchical structure of the N-mixture or occupancy model and include other hierarchical information (see Meehan’s recent work: <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.01581\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.01581&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792494601246000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1Fp4BtcQ01ehQT36qjTUMa\">https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.01581</a> ).</div><div><br></div><div>Our approach works for static abundance models but also with timing varying (currently AR1) and modeling varying spatiotemporally but the spatiotemporal requires a lot of data over many years based on our basic simulations.</div><div><br></div><div>Cheers,</div><div>Dan</div><div><br></div><div><div><b><font face=\"Garamond\">Daniel J. Hocking, Ph.D.</font></b></div><div><font face=\"Garamond\">Assistant Professor</font></div><div><font face=\"Garamond\"><a href=\"http://www.frostburg.edu/dept/biol/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.frostburg.edu/dept/biol/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792494601246000&amp;usg=AOvVaw34TYqEH7LAsSNIgdwfg5ly\">Department of Biology</a></font></div><div><font face=\"Garamond\">Frostburg State University</font></div><div><font face=\"Garamond\">Compton Science Center, room 309</font></div><div><font face=\"Garamond\">101 Braddock Road</font></div><div><font face=\"Garamond\">Frostburg, MD 21532-2303</font></div><div><span style=\"font-family:Garamond\">T. <a href=\"tel:(301)%20687-4343\" value=\"+13016874343\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\">301.687.4343</a></span></div><div><span style=\"font-family:Garamond,serif;font-size:9pt;color:blue\"><a href=\"http://hockinglab.weebly.com/\" style=\"font-family:Garamond,serif\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://hockinglab.weebly.com/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792494601246000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1FbcBMXAKEshYQ03UnYWfo\">Quantitative and Population Ecology Lab</a></span></div><div><br></div><div><br><blockquote type=\"cite\"></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div>On Nov 16, 2018, at 3:36 AM, Kery Marc &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">marc...@vogelwarte.ch</a>&gt; wrote:</div><br></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><div style=\"font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none;direction:ltr;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10pt\">Hi all,<br><br>motivated by an email from Jerry Cole, I would like to add the following:<br><br></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><div style=\"font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none;direction:ltr;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10pt\"><font size=\"2\">Blocking is really only the crudest form of accounting for similarity due to spatial proximity. On the other end of the spectrum are geostatistical models, such as these (in the context of &#39;interesting&#39; HMs):<br><a href=\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/51171238_Using_multilevel_spatial_models_to_understand_salamander_site_occupancy_patterns_after_wildfire\" style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/51171238_Using_multilevel_spatial_models_to_understand_salamander_site_occupancy_patterns_after_wildfire&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792494601246000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1RpldD4qgqr61NX25a-FUq\">https://www.researchgate.net/publication/51171238_Using_multilevel_spatial_models_to_understand_salamander_site_occupancy_patterns_after_wildfire</a><br><br>CAR models and geostatistical models with negative exponential correlation function can be fit with functions in WinBUGS or OpenBUGS, but not JAGS (you&#39;d have to code them up yourself).<br><br>With BUGS, splines are much more efficient though, see for instance here in the context of a static occupancy model:<a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1472-4642.2011.00831.x\" style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1472-4642.2011.00831.x&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792494601246000&amp;usg=AOvVaw02MDdwrPQjSMZNB4a0LTSQ\">https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1472-4642.2011.00831.x</a><br><br>If you are worried that blocking doesn&#39;t remove all the residual spatial autocorrelation, you could conceivably combine block effects (to eliminate larger-scale heterogeneity) with such more fine-scale modeling of residual correlations in the same model.<br><br>Finally, it&#39;s worth mentioning some R packages that can do (static) occupancy or Nmix models with modeling of residual spatial autocorrelation:<br></font></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><div style=\"font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none;direction:ltr;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10pt\"><font size=\"2\">-<span> </span></font><font size=\"2\"><font size=\"2\">For static occupancy models, there is the stocc R package, which does them with CAR and RSR, see<a href=\"https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1890/12-0564.1\" style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1890/12-0564.1&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792494601246000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1r9dSt-gaWikk1CFcEZWLc\">https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1890/12-0564.1</a><br></font></font></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><div style=\"font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none;direction:ltr;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10pt\"><font size=\"2\">- For static occupancy and Nmix models, there is the the hSDM package:<span> </span><a href=\"https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/hSDM/hSDM.pdf\" style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/hSDM/hSDM.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792494601246000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3nfMC7bNyrZm-U4SND49o9\">https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/hSDM/hSDM.pdf</a><br><br>Best regards  ---- Marc<br></font></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><div style=\"font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none;direction:ltr;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10pt\"><br><br><div style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;;font-size:16px\"><hr><div style=\"direction:ltr\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Tahoma\"><b>From:</b><span> </span>Kery Marc<br><b>Sent:</b><span> </span>12 November 2018 11:27<br><b>To:</b><span> </span><a href style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">hmec...@googlegroups.com</a><br><b>Subject:</b><span> </span>modeling spatial structure in HMs<br></font><br></div><div></div><div><div><div style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\">Dear all,</span></div><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"> </span></p><div style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\">Apologies for shameless self-PR here, but I think here’s a paper of some general (methodological) interest because it shows how spatial structure can easily be incorporated in HMs of the kinds that we talk about on this list. My colleagues had an observational study with a split-plot design: where there are nested levels of observation, with some covariates measured on one level and some on another. Just ignoring this inherent dependency would be bad (e.g., “pseudoreplication” and all that). The traditional way in mixed-model and ANOVA land to accommodate such spatial structure is to fit a random block effect to account for increased similarity of plots within the same block as opposed to those lying in different blocks. This is of course easily achieved in BUGS and here is an example inside of an abundance-based community model (i.e., a DRY model according to Chapter 11 in AHM1).</span></div><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"> </span></p><div style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\">To view the online publication, please click here:</span></div><div style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><a href=\"http://journal.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fevo.2018.00175/full?&amp;utm_source=Email_to_authors_&amp;utm_medium=Email&amp;utm_content=T1_11.5e1_author&amp;utm_campaign=Email_publication&amp;field=&amp;journalName=Frontiers_in_Ecology_and_Evolution&amp;id=349063\" style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://journal.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fevo.2018.00175/full?%26utm_source%3DEmail_to_authors_%26utm_medium%3DEmail%26utm_content%3DT1_11.5e1_author%26utm_campaign%3DEmail_publication%26field%3D%26journalName%3DFrontiers_in_Ecology_and_Evolution%26id%3D349063&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792494601246000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1ftI16AzhAOqzw770v9EVt\">http://journal.frontiersin.org/article/10.3389/fevo.2018.00175/full?&amp;utm_source=Email_to_authors_&amp;utm_medium=Email&amp;utm_content=T1_11.5e1_author&amp;utm_campaign=Email_publication&amp;field=&amp;journalName=Frontiers_in_Ecology_and_Evolution&amp;id=349063</a></span></div><p style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"> </span></p><div style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\">Of course, we’re not the first ones to do this, e.g., David Miller has a paper around 2013 where they accommodate such a blocking structure in a single-species occupancy model (I believe) and no doubt there must be others. But my point here is just to (1) emphasize that often we have such internal structure that is induced by the design of our study and (2) we can easily account for the resulting spatial structure.</span></div><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"> </span></p><div style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\">Bestr regards  --- Marc</span></div><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Tahoma,sans-serif\"> </span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Tahoma,sans-serif\"> </span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 0.0001pt;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"> </p></div></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><div style=\"font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none;direction:ltr;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10pt\"></div><p style=\"margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none\"></p><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none;float:none;display:inline!important\">--<span> </span></span><br style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none;float:none;display:inline!important\">*** Three hierarchical modeling email lists ***</span><br style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none;float:none;display:inline!important\">(1) unmarked: for questions specific to the R package unmarked</span><br style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none;float:none;display:inline!important\">(2) SCR: for design and Bayesian or non-bayesian analysis of spatial capture-recapture</span><br style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none;float:none;display:inline!important\">(3) HMecology (this list): for everything else, especially material covered in the books by Royle &amp; Dorazio (2008), Kéry &amp; Schaub (2012), Kéry &amp; Royle (2015)</span><br style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none;float:none;display:inline!important\">---<s